1
00:00:02,050 --> 00:00:03,750
人类文明
Human civilization

2
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:07,910
我们所了解、感觉、期待的一切
everything we can know,feel and hope for

3
00:00:08,030 --> 00:00:11,610
都是我们在地球上的
is for us here on Earth.

4
00:00:12,140 --> 00:00:15,910
区区八千英里宽的石头球体
A ball of rock, just 8 thousand miles wide.

5
00:00:16,970 --> 00:00:20,290
但是在那边，跨越广阔的天际
But out there, across the vast heavens

6
00:00:21,110 --> 00:00:24,380
可能存在着无数其他文明
there could be countless other civilizations

7
00:00:24,490 --> 00:00:30,480
每一个都拥有和我们大相径庭的知识、历史和志向
each with knowledge, history, and ambition vastly different from our own.

8
00:00:30,590 --> 00:00:33,570
直到一天，也许比想象来得要快
And one day,perhaps sooner than you think

9
00:00:33,690 --> 00:00:38,900
人类和外星文明将会碰撞
humanity and alien civilization will collide.

10
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:42,000
那一天将怎样地改变我们？
How will that day change us?

11
00:00:42,590 --> 00:00:45,170
那会否成为我们最伟大的跃进？
Will it be our greatest leap forward?

12
00:00:45,950 --> 00:00:49,180
还是我们了解的一切都将结束？
Or the end of everything we know?

13
00:00:54,450 --> 00:00:58,640
《同摩根·弗里曼一起穿越虫洞》
空间、时间、生命本身
Space, time, life itself.

14
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:05,290
宇宙的秘密，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

15
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:16,640
第一次接触，我们能否生还？

16
00:01:18,790 --> 00:01:21,930
想想你在学校学到的每件事
Think of everything you learned in school.

17
00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:26,140
想想爱因斯坦、佛、还有莎士比亚所知的一切
Think of everything that Einstein, Buddha and Shakespeare knew.

18
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:29,110
人类的全部经历
The entire experience of humanity

19
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:32,080
足以压倒淹没个人的思维
is overwhelming to a single mind.

20
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:34,470
但是如果还有外星人存在
But if aliens are out there,

21
00:01:34,580 --> 00:01:36,170
那么这些知识
all that is nothing

22
00:01:36,290 --> 00:01:40,590
和这个星系中的全部的知识相比，就是沧海一粟
compared with the sum total of knowledge in the galaxy.

23
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:44,510
我们和外星人第一次接触的那天
The day we make first contact with an alien race

24
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:48,090
将会是人类新时代的黎明
will be the dawn of a new era for humanity.

25
00:01:48,290 --> 00:01:53,090
它可能激发新的文艺复兴，并彻底改变我们
It could spur a renaissance that will change us utterly.

26
00:01:53,450 --> 00:01:58,980
或者，它将会是人类历史上最黑暗的一天
Or it could be the darkest day in human history.

27
00:02:02,630 --> 00:02:04,720
大多数四五十年代的孩子
Most kids in the '40s and '50s

28
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:08,130
读着在另一世界的探险故事长大
grew up reading tales of adventure on other worlds.

29
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:12,470
我们当中许多人都曾经幻想自己战斗在
And most of us imagined we were Earth's first line of defense

30
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:14,200
阻挡外星人入侵地球的第一线
against alien invaders.

31
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:19,590
那时，我更愿意觉得我们错了
These days, I would like to think we were wrong,

32
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:22,840
那些天外来客可能是友善的
that extraterrestrials might be friendly.

33
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:33,240
但是许多科学家相信，生物学的基本力量
But many scientists believe basic forces of biology

34
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:36,890
使得进攻性的外星人是个更安全的赌注
make aggressive aliens a safer bet.

35
00:02:40,130 --> 00:02:43,250
查尔斯·库克尔 是天体生物学教授
Charles Cockell is a Professor of Astrobiology

36
00:02:43,370 --> 00:02:46,190
在苏格兰爱丁堡大学
at the University of Edinburgh, Scotland.

37
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:49,490
他深入思考外星生命
He thinks deeply about alien life

38
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,800
以及进化如何决定他们在
and how evolution might determine their actions

39
00:02:52,910 --> 00:02:55,400
第一次接触时的表现
at first contact.

40
00:02:55,510 --> 00:02:57,230
不能仅因一个文明
It's not clear that a civilization

41
00:02:57,350 --> 00:02:59,300
能穿越太空旅行就
just because it can travel through space

42
00:02:59,410 --> 00:03:01,690
判定它们是无私的
would be altruistic.

43
00:03:03,490 --> 00:03:05,380
例如人类，
Humanity, for example,

44
00:03:05,490 --> 00:03:08,610
已经登陆了土卫六这颗冻结的卫星
landed spacecraft on the freezing moon of Titan,

45
00:03:08,730 --> 00:03:10,630
但我们仍在战争
and yet we still go to war.

46
00:03:11,830 --> 00:03:14,750
所以技术的进步并不会
So it's not the case that technological advancement

47
00:03:14,870 --> 00:03:17,760
同时带来无私和利他
necessarily is -- comes hand in hand with altruism,

48
00:03:17,870 --> 00:03:20,260
所以一个外星文明
and it's possible that an alien civilization

49
00:03:20,370 --> 00:03:22,300
可能是相当危险的
could actually be quite dangerous.

50
00:03:22,410 --> 00:03:25,230
查尔斯研究进化的力量
Charles studies the forces of evolution

51
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:27,670
这给予我们必要的智慧
that gave us the intelligence necessary

52
00:03:27,780 --> 00:03:29,170
成为高等文明
for an advanced civilization,

53
00:03:30,350 --> 00:03:34,870
他相信这同时会驱使外星人把我们撕成碎片
forces he believes could drive aliens to tear ours apart.

54
00:03:34,980 --> 00:03:38,740
这是一个关于自养生物和异养生物的问题
It's a question of autotrophs or heterotrophs,

55
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:41,970
或者用外行人的话说…
or in layman's terms...

56
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:44,270
晚饭吃什么？
What's for dinner?

57
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:46,280
自养生物是一种
An autotroph is a type of life

58
00:03:46,390 --> 00:03:48,950
利用光能的生物
that uses light as a source of energy.

59
00:03:49,060 --> 00:03:52,580
例如，这里的草就是自养生物
For example, the grass here is an autotrophic organism.

60
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:54,690
它们利用来自天空的阳光
It's using sunlight from the sky

61
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:57,320
来给自己提供能量，生长和繁殖
to power itself and to grow and reproduce.

62
00:03:57,430 --> 00:03:59,490
自养生物通常不具有智慧
Autotrophs generally are not intelligent.

63
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:02,430
收集阳光是一种收集能量的极好方式
Gathering sunlight is a very good way of gathering energy,

64
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:04,600
但却不可能收集足够的能量
but it never really gathers sufficient energy

65
00:04:04,710 --> 00:04:06,560
使其成为智慧生物
for a creature to become intelligent.

66
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:11,270
自养生物还有其他弱点
Autotrophs have another weakness.

67
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:14,910
它们容易被异养生物捕食
They're easy prey for heterotrophs.

68
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:16,810
我身后的羊群
These sheep behind me,

69
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:17,940
它们是异养生物
these are heterotrophs,

70
00:04:18,050 --> 00:04:19,710
他们是吃自养生物的
but they're feeding off autotrophs.

71
00:04:19,820 --> 00:04:22,310
它们吃田野中的草
They're feeding off the grass in this field.

72
00:04:22,610 --> 00:04:26,540
查尔斯相信，无论是地球生命
Charles believes that the food chain is just as real

73
00:04:26,650 --> 00:04:29,840
还是外星生命，食物链都是原力
of a force for alien life as it is for terrestrials.

74
00:04:31,060 --> 00:04:32,590
越聪明的生物
The smarter the creature,

75
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:35,920
就要消耗越多的食物来维持它们大脑工作
the more food it must consume to keep its brain working.

76
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:38,790
好的，谢谢。这正是我点的菜
All right. Thanks. Just what I ordered.

77
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:40,990
你看，我这里的开胃小菜
You can see the hors d'oeuvre I have here,

78
00:04:41,110 --> 00:04:43,160
你可以对草的量有一个理解
and you can get some idea of the amount of grass

79
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:45,580
我可以每天吃这么多来驱动一个羊的大脑
I would have to eat a day to power a sheep's brain.

80
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:48,870
但是当你需要驱动人的大脑时
But it's a completely different matter

81
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:51,050
就是另外一回事了
when you need to power a human brain.

82
00:04:55,790 --> 00:04:58,480
如果我们花费所有的工作时间
If we spent all of our waking hours

83
00:04:58,590 --> 00:05:00,080
来吃掉数磅的草
eating the pounds of grass

84
00:05:00,190 --> 00:05:03,590
用以来驱动大脑工作
it takes just to power our brain,

85
00:05:03,710 --> 00:05:07,500
那么我们就几乎没有时间来想像，
we would have little time for the imagination and ambition

86
00:05:07,610 --> 00:05:09,320
来实现把我们送上月球的抱负
that sent us to the Moon.

87
00:05:09,430 --> 00:05:11,290
好在自然提供了解决方法
But nature provides a solution.

88
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:17,130
并不太好，但是这是生命界的事实
It's not pretty, but it is a fact of life.

89
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:20,240
羊肉。谢谢。
Lamb. Thank you.

90
00:05:23,780 --> 00:05:25,630
我们巨大的大脑
Our massive brains

91
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:27,970
需要消耗大量的能量
require colossal amounts of energy.

92
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:31,260
成为最干净、最迅速的捕食者
And being a predator is the cleanest, quickest,

93
00:05:31,370 --> 00:05:34,110
是高等生物获取必要能量
and most effective way to acquire the energy necessary

94
00:05:34,220 --> 00:05:37,240
最行之有效的方法，
for advanced intelligence,

95
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:40,270
无论是人类还是外星人。
whether human or alien.

96
00:05:40,380 --> 00:05:41,730
智慧生物
Intelligent creatures,

97
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:43,740
特别是非常智慧的生物，都是肉食动物
particularly very intelligent creatures, would be predatory.

98
00:05:43,850 --> 00:05:46,150
它们位于食物链的末尾
They would be at the end of a food chain,

99
00:05:46,260 --> 00:05:46,800
它们会吃掉其他生物
and they would eat other creatures

100
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:48,070
由于它们需要吃掉其他生物
that would eat other creatures.

101
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:49,840
所以他们必定是掠夺者
So they're bound to be predatory in some way.

102
00:05:51,120 --> 00:05:55,410
查尔斯确信，第一次接触
Charles is confident that at first contact,

103
00:05:55,530 --> 00:05:59,180
是捕食者之间的竞赛
we will meet an intelligent race of predators,

104
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:04,320
捕食者几乎都会成为好战的生物
predators that most likely developed a war-like culture

105
00:06:04,440 --> 00:06:06,840
就像我们一样。
just like ours.

106
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:08,450
人类之间的冲突
Conflict in human beings

107
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:09,750
明确地与掠夺有关
is definitely linked to predation.

108
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:12,020
关于领土的贪婪
It's all about grasping territory.

109
00:06:12,130 --> 00:06:15,750
对于资源的占有和支配
It's all about having resources and commanding those resources

110
00:06:15,870 --> 00:06:18,390
在饥荒或其他困难时期。
in times of a famine or in times of difficulty.

111
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:22,690
出现在我们门前的外星捕食者
Predatory aliens who show up on our doorstep

112
00:06:22,810 --> 00:06:25,830
可能是需要新的资源
may well be in need of new resources.

113
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:30,250
如果这样，人类的生存就置于危险之中
If so, the existence of humanity could be in jeopardy.

114
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:33,610
如果他们需要来烹调他们食物的氧气
If they need oxygen to burn their food,

115
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:36,310
就像我们需要氧气来烹制我们的食物一样
just as in the same way we need oxygen to burn our food,

116
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:38,510
它们会发现这个星球上的自然条件
they might find the environmental conditions

117
00:06:38,620 --> 00:06:41,080
对于他们的文明来说太有益了
on this planet very conducive for their civilization.

118
00:06:41,190 --> 00:06:44,060
一个像我们这样含氧的星球
And oxygen-bearing planets like ours

119
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:45,180
大气层中有着丰富的氧含量
with high levels of oxygen in the atmosphere

120
00:06:45,290 --> 00:06:47,380
在宇宙中可能相当稀有
might actually be quite rare in the universe.

121
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:51,140
一旦发现，就是他们要去的好地方
And when you find them, they might be good places to go.

122
00:06:51,250 --> 00:06:54,420
我们常在电影中看到这样的情节
It is a scenario we usually see in the movies.

123
00:06:55,530 --> 00:06:58,450
外星人如同无敌征服舰队而来
Aliens arrive as a conquering armada,

124
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:03,060
人类不顾一切地为生存而战
and mankind engages them in a desperate battle for survival.

125
00:07:03,170 --> 00:07:07,860
但是一个科学家表示，恕不同意
But one scientist begs to differ.

126
00:07:07,970 --> 00:07:10,500
提到一个怀有敌意的文明
Let's say that a civilization is hostile.

127
00:07:10,610 --> 00:07:13,900
好莱坞已经给我们洗脑，想到
Hollywood has brainwashed us into thinking

128
00:07:14,010 --> 00:07:16,870
那将会是大卫和哥利亚（圣经中被大卫杀死的巨人）
that it's gonna be a Titanic battle

129
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:18,940
之间惊心动魄的战斗
between David and Goliath.

130
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:21,530
我的意思是，等一下
I mean, give me a break.

131
00:07:21,650 --> 00:07:26,270
那会更像是小鹿斑比和哥斯拉之间进行
More than likely, it's gonna be between Bambi and Godzilla.

132
00:07:26,590 --> 00:07:31,080
纽约市立大学的物理学家 加来道雄（日裔美籍）
Physicist Michio Kaku of the City College of New York

133
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:33,420
试图更科学的评价
tries to be more scientific

134
00:07:33,530 --> 00:07:37,620
关于人类和外星文明之间的关系
about mankind's relationship to alien civilizations.

135
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:41,360
我们物理学家喜欢将外星文明
We physicists like to classify alien civilizations

136
00:07:41,470 --> 00:07:43,260
按照能量分类
in outer space by energy.

137
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:47,000
第一类文明掌控行星的能量
A type-one civilization harnesses planetary power.

138
00:07:47,110 --> 00:07:51,200
例如，它们可以控制飓风
They can, for example, control hurricanes,

139
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:53,310
控制火山
control volcanoes.

140
00:07:53,420 --> 00:07:56,920
第二类文明可以控制整个恒星
Type two can control an entire star.

141
00:07:57,540 --> 00:07:59,300
他们和恒星游戏
They play with stars.

142
00:07:59,580 --> 00:08:05,310
第三类控制整个星系的能量
Type three controls the power of an entire galaxy.

143
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:09,180
在这个宇宙标度的衡量下，我们在哪？
On this cosmic scale of energy, what are we?

144
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:12,620
我们能控制飓风和火山么？
Do we control hurricanes and volcanoes?

145
00:08:12,730 --> 00:08:14,560
我们能和恒星游戏么？
Do we play with stars?

146
00:08:14,670 --> 00:08:18,490
我们能在横跨星系的车道漫步么？
Do we roam across the space lanes of the galaxy?

147
00:08:18,610 --> 00:08:21,300
不能。我们是第零类
No. We are type zero.

148
00:08:21,410 --> 00:08:23,540
我们在这个标度上根本排不上号
We don't even rate on the scale.

149
00:08:25,150 --> 00:08:27,770
但是终究我们可能还有生存机会
But we may have a chance to survive

150
00:08:27,880 --> 00:08:29,940
在银河丛林中
in the galactic jungle after all.

151
00:08:30,050 --> 00:08:33,540
加来认为我们可以提升我们的能力等级
Michio believes that we can rise in the ranks of civilization

152
00:08:33,650 --> 00:08:36,440
通过学习我们优秀的邻居
by learning from our superior neighbors.

153
00:08:36,780 --> 00:08:40,200
获取他们知识的最安全方法…
And the safest way to acquire their knowledge...

154
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:43,140
就是窃听
Is eavesdropping.

155
00:08:43,250 --> 00:08:45,840
外星人也得遵守物理法则
Even aliens have to obey the laws of physics,

156
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:49,380
物理法则告诉我们，
and the laws of physics tell us that electromagnetic radiation

157
00:08:49,490 --> 00:08:51,420
电磁辐射是最快、最方便的方式
is the fastest, most convenient way

158
00:08:51,530 --> 00:08:53,150
来进行星际通信
to communicate between stars.

159
00:08:54,570 --> 00:08:58,560
天际之间充满了电磁辐射的嗡嗡声
The heavens are buzzing with electromagnetic radiation.

160
00:08:58,670 --> 00:09:01,220
绝大多数是自然产生的
Almost all of it occurs naturally.

161
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:03,890
但在地球周围，大部分是人造的
And around Earth, most is manmade.

162
00:09:04,010 --> 00:09:08,160
但是在这宁静中，可能夹杂着
But buried in all that static could be some radiation

163
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:11,650
外星人用来传输信息的辐射
that aliens are using to send a message.

164
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:16,110
寻找这样的一个外星信号非常困难
Finding such an alien signal is really tough,

165
00:09:16,220 --> 00:09:18,380
就像在纽约市的
like looking for a single car

166
00:09:18,490 --> 00:09:23,260
车水马龙之中试图寻找一辆汽车
in the swarm of traffic that
clogs the streets of New York City.

167
00:09:24,130 --> 00:09:27,000
我们通过电磁辐射来发送信息
We send messages by electromagnetic radiation,

168
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:30,740
无论是电子邮件、无线电、雷达
whether it's in the form of e-mail, radio, radar,

169
00:09:30,850 --> 00:09:32,610
微波、调频或者调幅
microwaves, F.M., or A.M.

170
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:35,380
就像是一辆把一辆代表信息的车
Let's say that this car represents a message

171
00:09:35,490 --> 00:09:37,080
发送到太空
that we send into outer space.

172
00:09:37,190 --> 00:09:40,950
大部分信息都是你我、人类家庭发送的
Most messages are sent by you and me, by the human family.

173
00:09:41,070 --> 00:09:44,690
但是我们所寻找的更加罕见
However, what I'm looking for is much more rare.

174
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:46,320
我们在寻找这个——
I'm looking for this --

175
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:49,390
一条由外星文明使用电磁辐射
a message sent by an alien civilization

176
00:09:49,510 --> 00:09:51,760
发送的消息
using electromagnetic radiation.

177
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:55,210
在过去的四十年里
For the past 40 years,

178
00:09:55,330 --> 00:09:57,350
射电望远镜巨大的军团
armies of large radio telescopes

179
00:09:57,460 --> 00:10:00,320
调谐在宇宙的频率上
have tuned into the frequencies of the Universe,

180
00:10:00,430 --> 00:10:03,990
正如调谐收音机旋钮来寻找广播电台
just like turning the radio dial to find a station.

181
00:10:04,100 --> 00:10:07,190
目前为止，科学家所听到的，只有空空的宁静
So far, scientists have heard only empty static.

182
00:10:07,630 --> 00:10:11,390
但是加来认为，他们收听的方法
But Michio believes that is because they have been listening

183
00:10:11,510 --> 00:10:12,510
不对路
the wrong way.

184
00:10:12,870 --> 00:10:16,410
我们同一时间只能监听无数个
We are only listening to one frequency at a time

185
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:19,640
可能的频率中的一个
among an infinite number of possible frequencies.

186
00:10:19,750 --> 00:10:22,410
那就行试图通过在公路边拍照的方法
It's like trying to find this alien decal

187
00:10:22,520 --> 00:10:26,160
寻找一辆贴有外星人贴纸的汽车
on a car on a freeway by taking pictures.

188
00:10:30,990 --> 00:10:33,410
那要花费我多长的时间
How long would it take for me

189
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:36,240
来找到这个外星人贴纸呢？
to finally identify that alien decal?

190
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:38,250
一周？几个月？数年？
A week? Months? Years?

191
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:40,730
也许永远找不到。
Maybe even never.

192
00:10:40,850 --> 00:10:41,650
加来相信
Michio believes

193
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:45,020
即使我们真遇到一个外星人发来的信息
that even if we stumble upon an elusive alien message,

194
00:10:45,130 --> 00:10:46,790
我们可能不会认出来它
we might not even recognize it.

195
00:10:46,900 --> 00:10:48,560
发现这样罕见信号
There may still be hope

196
00:10:48,670 --> 00:10:51,690
的希望依然存在，然而
to discover these rare signals, however,

197
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:54,960
需要我们来扩大视野
if we broaden our perspective.

198
00:10:55,070 --> 00:10:57,900
假设我想发送这条外星消息
Let's say that I want to send this alien message

199
00:10:58,010 --> 00:11:00,690
横跨外太空
across outer space.

200
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:02,630
假设每辆车都代表一个光子
And let's say that each car represents a photon

201
00:11:02,750 --> 00:11:05,730
光子振荡在特定的频率
vibrating at a certain frequency.

202
00:11:05,840 --> 00:11:09,680
那么，发送这样一条信息的最傻的方法
Well, perhaps the silliest way to send such a message

203
00:11:09,790 --> 00:11:14,170
就是把整个信息都放在一个频率上
is to put the entire message on one photon at one frequency.

204
00:11:14,290 --> 00:11:17,630
信息可能会丢失，信息可能会退化
The message could be lost.The message could be degraded.

205
00:11:17,750 --> 00:11:19,260
一种更高效的方法是
A more efficient way

206
00:11:19,370 --> 00:11:23,010
把信息分散在不同的频率中
is to cut up the message into different frequencies,

207
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:25,500
创立一种叫做 宽带通信 的方法
creating what is called a broadband,

208
00:11:25,620 --> 00:11:31,550
你把一部分信息放在每一个光子上
and then you put each fragment of the message on each photon.

209
00:11:32,530 --> 00:11:35,390
使用多通道来发送信息
Using multiple channels to send a message

210
00:11:35,510 --> 00:11:40,580
会更快，就像宽带比拨号上网要快一样。
is faster in the same way broadband is faster than dial-up.

211
00:11:40,950 --> 00:11:43,670
如果外星人通过这种方式来通信
But if aliens communicate this way,

212
00:11:43,790 --> 00:11:46,830
它们的信号会更难被察觉
their signals would be harder to detect.

213
00:11:47,520 --> 00:11:48,520
现在，相反的是
Now, here's the irony.

214
00:11:48,630 --> 00:11:52,280
如果你在窃听外星人的通信
If you were to eavesdrop on an alien conversation

215
00:11:52,390 --> 00:11:54,650
你捡到的只是这些碎片
and you would pick up all these pieces,

216
00:11:54,760 --> 00:11:56,350
你只会听到无用数据
you would just hear gibberish.

217
00:11:57,320 --> 00:11:58,640
所以换句话说
So in other words,

218
00:11:58,760 --> 00:12:02,710
我们现在可能就处于一个跨银河系对话链路之间
we could be in the middle of an ntergalactic conversation

219
00:12:02,830 --> 00:12:04,320
却不知道它
and not even know it.

220
00:12:05,120 --> 00:12:07,680
如果加来是对的
If Michio is correct,

221
00:12:07,790 --> 00:12:11,420
星际间可能充满了外星人的对话
the heavens could be filled with alien chatter.

222
00:12:11,530 --> 00:12:13,450
我们没有看到这些无线电交通
We do not see this radio traffic

223
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:15,380
是因为我们的单频率搜索
because our signal-frequency search

224
00:12:15,490 --> 00:12:18,610
仅仅收到了他们对话的破碎片段
only picks up fragments of their conversations.

225
00:12:19,300 --> 00:12:22,890
但是如果我们宽带地看
But if we were to look at the broadband,

226
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:27,180
我们可能会发现一直存在的某些东西
we might find what has been there all along.

227
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:34,070
如果我们探测到了外星通信
If we do learn to detect alien communication,

228
00:12:34,180 --> 00:12:37,140
我们有没有希望能听懂它？
how can we ever hope to understand it?

229
00:12:37,250 --> 00:12:39,380
他们讲什么语言？
What language would they speak?

230
00:12:39,490 --> 00:12:41,880
现在，一个科学家相信
Now, one scientist believes

231
00:12:41,990 --> 00:12:44,480
他知道如何来突破任何一种语言
he knows how to crack the code of any language,

232
00:12:44,590 --> 00:12:47,660
无论是不是外星语言
no matter how alien.

233
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:52,660
世界上
Around the world,

234
00:12:53,180 --> 00:12:57,880
我们使用将近7000种语言相互交谈
we speak to each other in about 7,000 different languages.

235
00:12:57,990 --> 00:13:01,250
我们中大多数人懂得其中的一两种
Most of us understand only one or two.

236
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:04,680
但是没有无法被翻译的现存语言
But there is no living language that cannot be translated,

237
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:08,150
因为它们具有一个共同点——
because they all have one thing in common --

238
00:13:09,090 --> 00:13:11,810
人类大脑
the human brain.

239
00:13:11,930 --> 00:13:15,120
外星大脑会创造什么语言？
What languages might alien brains create?

240
00:13:15,230 --> 00:13:18,220
我们能否指望懂得它们？
Could we ever hope to understand them?

241
00:13:18,630 --> 00:13:21,950
我们甚至究竟能否以语言的形式
Could we even recognize their communication

242
00:13:22,070 --> 00:13:24,230
意识到他们的交流？
as language at all?

243
00:13:26,370 --> 00:13:30,340
事实上我只会讲英语和一点斯瓦希里语
Well, I actually only speak English and Swahili a little bit.

244
00:13:30,580 --> 00:13:32,600
我还学过一点纳瓦霍语
I've learned a little Navajo,

245
00:13:32,710 --> 00:13:35,070
我还能写一点彻罗基语
and I can write a little bit in Cherokee.

246
00:13:35,180 --> 00:13:38,910
哦，还有俄语，我学过一两年
Oh, I took Russian, too, for a couple years.

247
00:13:39,230 --> 00:13:42,260
劳伦斯·多伊是一个通信专家
Laurance Doyle is a communications expert

248
00:13:42,370 --> 00:13:44,220
在 地外智慧生物搜索学会
with the Institute of the Search

249
00:13:44,340 --> 00:13:46,930
缩写SETI
for Extraterrestrial Intelligence, or SETI.

250
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:51,500
他的工作是在他的列表中添加另一个语言
He is working to add one more language to his list,

251
00:13:51,610 --> 00:13:55,700
一个没有任何人类讲过的语言
one no human has ever spoken.

252
00:13:55,810 --> 00:13:57,640
宽吻海豚，好极了
Bottle-nose dolphins are perfect.

253
00:13:57,750 --> 00:13:58,770
我们知道它们是有智慧的
We know they're intelligent.

254
00:13:58,880 --> 00:13:59,900
认真的，咬牙声
Seriously, Chomp.

255
00:14:00,020 --> 00:14:01,440
哇，那是有趣的
Wow, that's interesting.

256
00:14:01,550 --> 00:14:03,880
它们通过非常复杂的方式交流
They communicate in very complex ways.

257
00:14:03,990 --> 00:14:06,080
它们制造气泡，它们嬉戏
They make bubbles.They play.

258
00:14:06,190 --> 00:14:07,710
它们有着复杂的社会
They have complex society.

259
00:14:07,830 --> 00:14:10,520
但是我们没有任何相同的参考系统
But we don't have any common reference system.

260
00:14:10,630 --> 00:14:12,150
我们之间无法谈论电影
We can't talk about movies.

261
00:14:12,260 --> 00:14:15,890
我们之间无法谈论哪怕是最基础的事情
We can't talk about the most elementary things.

262
00:14:19,790 --> 00:14:21,450
劳伦斯决定来看看
Laurance decided to see

263
00:14:21,560 --> 00:14:25,380
他是否能从海豚的话语之中得到任何有效信息
whether he could extract any meaning from dolphin speak.

264
00:14:33,570 --> 00:14:36,360
因为它们交谈之间
Because they have this huge range

265
00:14:36,470 --> 00:14:38,670
有这么大的幅度
over which they can communicate.

266
00:14:42,860 --> 00:14:45,180
当你观察这些哨声间的关系时
When you look at the relationship of whistles,

267
00:14:45,300 --> 00:14:48,990
一种结构出现了，就像
a structure starts to emerge that's not unlike syntax

268
00:14:49,110 --> 00:14:50,750
人类语言中的句法
in human languages.

269
00:14:50,870 --> 00:14:54,850
但是这些哨声和敲击声是否确实一种
But are these whistles and clicks an actual language,

270
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:57,410
可被翻译的语言呢？
one that could be translated?

271
00:14:58,240 --> 00:15:01,210
劳伦斯研究了这些声音的模式
Laurance searched for patterns in the sounds

272
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:05,320
使用一种叫做 信息论 的统计方法
using a statistical technique called information theory,

273
00:15:05,940 --> 00:15:10,370
一种足以在大海般的噪音中捞到
a theory powerful enough to find a needle of meaning

274
00:15:10,480 --> 00:15:13,010
针一样的信息的有力工具
in a haystack noise.

275
00:15:14,230 --> 00:15:16,280
信息论的建立是基于
Information theory is built on the probability

276
00:15:16,390 --> 00:15:18,740
给定信号出现的概率——
of occurrence of a given signal.

277
00:15:18,860 --> 00:15:21,170
它出现得有多么频繁？
How often does it occur,

278
00:15:21,290 --> 00:15:22,820
以及它的出现时，它和其他信号之间的
and how often does it have a relationship

279
00:15:22,930 --> 00:15:25,600
相关有多么频繁？
with other signals in that occurrence?

280
00:15:25,970 --> 00:15:29,190
“英仙座最好祝愿老虎奶牛
"Perseus best wishes tiger cow

281
00:15:29,300 --> 00:15:33,630
接受印刷墨水水疗蜜鹦鹉铲…”
accepting printing ink hydro lories shovel in..."

282
00:15:33,740 --> 00:15:36,720
这是一种单词的随机分布
So, this is kind of a random distribution of words.

283
00:15:37,330 --> 00:15:41,600
我可以相加算出每个单词出现的频率
I can add up how frequently each word occurs.

284
00:15:41,710 --> 00:15:44,820
我从随机分布的单词中得到的是
What I am going to get from this random distribution of words

285
00:15:44,930 --> 00:15:48,840
每个单词以几乎相等的频率出现
is that each word occurs with about the same equal frequency.

286
00:15:49,250 --> 00:15:50,870
在这篇胡言乱语中
In this page of gibberish,

287
00:15:50,980 --> 00:15:54,370
画出单词和其出现频率的图表
plotting a chart of words against how often they are used

288
00:15:54,490 --> 00:15:56,860
是一条直线
gives us a straight line.

289
00:15:57,900 --> 00:16:02,790
这意味着事实上没有语言信息被发送
That means there's virtually no linguistic
information being transmitted.

290
00:18:10,110 --> 00:18:13,330
哪些包含了要寻找的信息
and which has the information we're looking for,

291
00:18:13,450 --> 00:18:15,980
即智慧生命的信号
has a sign of intelligence.

292
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:37,660
这也是之所以要在地球上非人类物种上练习
And that's why you want to practice on non-human species on Earth.

293
00:18:43,380 --> 00:18:46,050
我认为那是海豚在说“一会儿见”
I think that was dolphin for "see you later."

294
00:18:48,350 --> 00:18:50,310
但是我们也许还需等待
But we may not have to wait

295
00:18:50,420 --> 00:18:55,240
等待收听到第一次接触的外星信息
to intercept an alien message to make first contact.

296
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:58,780
许多科学家现在怀疑，一个先进的飞船
Many scientists now suspect an advanced spacecraft

297
00:18:58,890 --> 00:19:03,200
很快将会建立起两个世界间直接的联系。
will soon forge a direct link between two worlds.

298
00:19:04,110 --> 00:19:08,100
而且这个飞船可能来自…
And that spacecraft could come...

299
00:19:09,370 --> 00:19:10,920
地球
From Earth.

300
00:19:13,850 --> 00:19:16,070
在1903年
In 1903,

301
00:19:16,180 --> 00:19:20,430
奥维尔·莱特引领了人类历史上首次动力飞行
Orville Wright guided the first powered flight in human history.

302
00:19:20,540 --> 00:19:23,350
他仅飞过了100英尺（30米）
He traveled just over 100 feet.

303
00:19:23,460 --> 00:19:26,670
仅仅66年之后
Only 66 years later,

304
00:19:26,780 --> 00:19:29,180
三个人飞越了25万英里（40万公里）
three men flew a quarter of a million miles

305
00:19:29,290 --> 00:19:31,200
并在月球上着陆
and landed on the Moon.

306
00:19:31,870 --> 00:19:36,170
下一次巨大航行突破可能即将到来
The next quantum leap in travel may be close at hand.

307
00:19:36,280 --> 00:19:40,070
将我们带到离地球万亿英里之外
It would take us trillions of miles from Earth,

308
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:45,100
我们能在外星人发现我们之前，找到他们
and we could find aliens before they find us.

309
00:19:46,070 --> 00:19:50,680
这应部分归功于这个人
And that is thanks in part to this man.

310
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:57,750
约翰·布洛菲，工作于NASA的喷气推进实验室，加州帕萨迪纳
John Brophy works at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Pasadena, California.

311
00:19:58,440 --> 00:20:00,110
他的专业是…
His expertise...

312
00:20:00,920 --> 00:20:03,240
先进推进
Is exotic propulsion.

313
00:20:05,470 --> 00:20:08,590
这是一个普通的气球，我将在头发上摩擦它
So, this is an ordinary balloon, and I'm gonna rub it on my hair,

314
00:20:08,710 --> 00:20:10,760
这会分离一些电荷
which will separate some of the charges,

315
00:20:10,870 --> 00:20:13,300
在气球上留下一些净电荷
leaving a net charge on the balloon,

316
00:20:13,410 --> 00:20:15,600
使得它可以粘在我的衬衣上
which will allow it to stick on my shirt.

317
00:20:21,340 --> 00:20:24,260
和把气球粘在纺织品上一样，这种力
The same force that holds a balloon to a piece of fabric

318
00:20:24,370 --> 00:20:29,180
也可以反过来用于推动飞船
can be used in reverse to push a vessel through space.

319
00:20:29,610 --> 00:20:31,890
我们刚刚阐明
So, as we just illustrated where we can use

320
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:35,660
相反的带电粒子可以相互吸引
oppositely charged particles to attract each other,

321
00:20:35,780 --> 00:20:37,610
如果我们使用相同的电荷
if we use particles with the same charges,

322
00:20:37,730 --> 00:20:39,730
它们将互相排斥
we can actually repel them.

323
00:20:41,310 --> 00:20:43,930
这种电排斥
This electrical repulsion

324
00:20:44,050 --> 00:20:48,570
就是约翰革命性的机器的核心，离子发动机
is the heart of John's revolutionary machine, the Ion engine.

325
00:20:49,200 --> 00:20:53,460
氙气电离后带有正电荷
Xenon gas is ionized with a positive charge,

326
00:20:53,580 --> 00:20:58,630
放置于带有正电荷的引擎机身
then placed against the engine's positively charged body.

327
00:20:59,510 --> 00:21:02,330
电荷相互排斥
And those like charges repel each other

328
00:21:02,440 --> 00:21:04,800
将离子以高速喷出
and push the ions out at a very high speed.

329
00:21:04,910 --> 00:21:06,400
通过喷出离子
And by pushing the ions out,

330
00:21:06,520 --> 00:21:09,110
将飞船像相反方向推动
it pushes the spacecraft in the other direction.

331
00:21:09,220 --> 00:21:12,410
NASA最近计划安排约翰的离子引擎
NASA recently charted John's Ion engine

332
00:21:12,520 --> 00:21:17,150
将黎明号飞船送上二十亿英里的旅程
to send the Dawn spacecraft on a two-billion-mile journey

333
00:21:17,260 --> 00:21:19,720
前往小行星带
to the asteroid belt.

334
00:21:19,830 --> 00:21:24,050
引擎持续工作超过三年
The engine stayed lit for more than three years.

335
00:21:24,380 --> 00:21:28,600
正是这开天辟地的成就
This groundbreaking achievement...

336
00:21:28,710 --> 00:21:33,810
使得同样高性能工程发展成为可能
was made possible by the same high-performance engineering

337
00:21:34,080 --> 00:21:37,850
势如破竹，产生出锐不可挡、毫不松懈
found in the unbridled, uncompromising,

338
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:41,880
气贯长虹的能量，供给全美国的…
unrelenting power of an all-American...

339
00:21:45,880 --> 00:21:47,630
…高尔夫球车
...golf cart.

340
00:21:48,690 --> 00:21:51,080
这两台车之间将要进行一场比赛
We're gonna have a race between these two vehicles.

341
00:21:51,440 --> 00:21:54,730
看似不公平的比赛，但是事实上
And it looks like an unfair race, but actually,

342
00:21:54,840 --> 00:21:57,570
我们将要给两台车提供同等数量的燃料
we're gonna give each vehicle the same amount of fuel,

343
00:21:57,680 --> 00:22:01,480
来看两台车能开多远，开多快
and we're gonna see how far and how fast these two vehicles go.

344
00:22:12,410 --> 00:22:16,180
不同于将我们送上月球的化学火箭
Unlike the fuel-guzzling chemical rockets that took us to the moon,

345
00:22:16,290 --> 00:22:21,010
黎明号飞船是非常节能的运输工具
the Dawn spacecraft is an extremely fuel-efficient vehicle.

346
00:22:21,120 --> 00:22:23,950
但是，和高尔夫球车一样
But, like the golf cart,

347
00:22:24,060 --> 00:22:26,870
它行动有些迟缓
it's a bit slow off the mark.

348
00:22:27,600 --> 00:22:29,010
黎明号飞船
The Dawn spacecraft,

349
00:22:29,120 --> 00:22:31,280
就像这样慢慢地加速
it accelerates very slowly like we've done.

350
00:22:31,390 --> 00:22:35,760
事实上，它从零加速到60英里每小时（96km/h）需要大约四天
In fact, it'll go 0 to 60 miles an hour in about four days.

351
00:22:35,870 --> 00:22:38,760
没有什么值得大书特书
So, not something you'd write home about.

352
00:22:38,870 --> 00:22:40,600
但是，四年之后
But, after four years,

353
00:22:40,710 --> 00:22:43,520
它将加速到超过26000英里每小时（11km/s）
it'll be going over 26,000 miles an hour

354
00:22:43,630 --> 00:22:46,240
仅消耗60加仑（0.22立方米）的燃料
and have used only 60 gallons of fuel.

355
00:22:46,350 --> 00:22:49,590
它及其高效，节省燃料
It is exquisitely fuel-efficient.

356
00:22:49,700 --> 00:22:53,660
老式的化学火箭加速更快，但燃料也将很快耗尽
The old chemical rocket gets going faster, but it burns out quickly.

357
00:22:53,770 --> 00:22:55,880
然而，一个离子宇宙飞船
An ion-powered spacecraft, however,

358
00:22:55,990 --> 00:22:58,930
可以保持加速长达数年
can keep on accelerating for years...

359
00:23:01,030 --> 00:23:05,150
并最终将比任何一种化学火箭
...and eventually travel faster and farther

360
00:23:05,260 --> 00:23:08,010
飞得更快、更远
than a chemical rocket ever could.

361
00:23:08,120 --> 00:23:12,040
使用化学火箭完成恒星间的旅行
It's completely impractical to use chemical-rocket technology

362
00:23:12,150 --> 00:23:14,550
完全不切实际，因为
to travel to the stars, and that's because

363
00:23:14,660 --> 00:23:18,850
火箭无法携带足够的推进剂
you just cannot carry enough propellent to make that trip.

364
00:23:20,190 --> 00:23:24,570
最近的恒星——比邻星
The closest star, Proxima Centauri,

365
00:23:25,040 --> 00:23:28,500
大约25万亿英里之遥
is about 25 trillion miles away.

366
00:23:29,440 --> 00:23:32,370
那将是漫长的旅途
That is a very long journey.

367
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:35,910
想像从太阳在这个25美分硬币中间
Imagine the sun at the center of this quarter

368
00:23:36,020 --> 00:23:39,730
冥王星的轨道在硬币的边缘
and Pluto's orbit around the outside edge of the quarter.

369
00:23:39,850 --> 00:23:42,250
你在学校中学到的每个行星
Every planet that you learned about in school,

370
00:23:42,360 --> 00:23:46,410
你知道的每个人，都在这枚硬币上
everybody that you know is contained within this quarter.

371
00:23:46,520 --> 00:23:48,850
利用现在的电子推进技术
With our current electric-propulsion technology

372
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:51,990
或者再做一些小小的改进
or advances that we can readily make,

373
00:23:52,110 --> 00:23:55,820
我们就能相对容易地在这片区域里遨游
we can get around this region of space relatively well.

374
00:23:55,930 --> 00:24:00,010
真正要看的是它如何去最近的恒星
What we really want to do is see what it takes to get to the nearest star.

375
00:24:00,120 --> 00:24:02,580
问题在于，最近的恒星在哪？
The question is, where is that?

376
00:24:20,260 --> 00:24:22,690
这里就是最近的恒星
And this is the closest star.

377
00:24:24,500 --> 00:24:26,170
天文学家现在相信
Astronomers now believe

378
00:24:26,290 --> 00:24:31,620
在地球周围一百万亿英里的范围内，有17个类太阳系
there are 17 alien solar systems
within 100 trillion miles of Earth.

379
00:24:32,610 --> 00:24:34,900
通过一些工程改进
With some engineering improvements,

380
00:24:35,020 --> 00:24:37,030
约翰的离子引擎的升级版
future versions of John's Ion engine

381
00:24:37,140 --> 00:24:39,720
能够到达这些新世界中任何一个
could reach any of these new worlds.

382
00:24:39,830 --> 00:24:42,170
但是在可预知的未来
But for the foreseeable future,

383
00:24:42,280 --> 00:24:45,150
离子动力飞船没有能力
an ion-powered vessel will not have the power

384
00:24:45,270 --> 00:24:47,970
载人完成这段旅程
to take human beings along for the ride.

385
00:24:48,080 --> 00:24:51,110
送往恒星的载荷要很小
The payloads have to be small to go to the stars

386
00:24:51,230 --> 00:24:54,050
因为必须要将这质量加速到
primarily because you have to accelerate that mass

387
00:24:54,170 --> 00:24:55,510
很高的速度
to a very high speed,

388
00:24:55,620 --> 00:24:57,980
飞船越轻
and the lighter weight the vehicle is,

389
00:24:58,090 --> 00:24:59,900
越容易实现
the easier that is to do.

390
00:25:01,690 --> 00:25:03,340
一天
One day,

391
00:25:03,460 --> 00:25:06,090
外星飞船终将穿破云霄
an alien spacecraft will pierce the clouds

392
00:25:06,200 --> 00:25:08,460
通向未知的世界
of an unexplored world.

393
00:25:09,730 --> 00:25:13,330
而外星人，可能就是我们
And the aliens might be us.

394
00:25:13,610 --> 00:25:16,000
飞越浩渺的宇宙空间
Crossing the vast emptiness of space

395
00:25:16,110 --> 00:25:19,470
并不是完成第一次接触的唯一挑战
is not the only challenge of making first contact.

396
00:25:20,230 --> 00:25:24,960
我们必须知道当我们到达那里时，该说些什么
We must also figure out what we're going to say when we get there.

397
00:25:28,530 --> 00:25:32,060
如何跨过语言障碍而交流？
How does one communicate across language barriers?

398
00:25:32,530 --> 00:25:37,840
地球上，我们的表情可以表达恐惧、爱或者欢乐
Here on Earth, our faces can express fear, love, or happiness

399
00:25:37,950 --> 00:25:40,080
有时效果甚至比词语更好
even better than words sometimes.

400
00:25:40,750 --> 00:25:44,610
但是我们如何才能和外星人交流感情
But what ideas and emotions could we share with aliens,

401
00:25:45,500 --> 00:25:49,250
并且避免传达错误的信息？
and how can we avoid sending the wrong message?

402
00:25:51,260 --> 00:25:54,790
有一个驻伊美军的故事
There's a story from U.S. soldiers in Iraq.

403
00:25:55,050 --> 00:25:58,260
它们要一辆车在检查站停下
They wanted cars to stop at a checkpoint.

404
00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:02,380
他们使用了在他们看来通用的“停止”手势
They used what they thought was a universal symbol for "stop"

405
00:26:02,490 --> 00:26:04,710
抬高并举起手掌
by holding up their out-raised palm.

406
00:26:04,820 --> 00:26:07,910
不幸的是，在伊拉克
Unfortunately for the people in Iraq,

407
00:26:08,020 --> 00:26:12,420
这个手势也表示“欢迎”，而不是“停止”
this is the symbol also for "welcome," not "stop."

408
00:26:12,540 --> 00:26:14,770
汽车继续驶向检查点
So the cars kept approaching the checkpoint

409
00:26:14,890 --> 00:26:17,370
甚至尽管士兵
even though the soldiers

410
00:26:17,480 --> 00:26:20,900
强烈地表示让他们停下
were signaling violently for them to stop.

411
00:26:24,680 --> 00:26:29,220
吉姆·卡卡里奥斯 是一位探索科幻小说背后的
Jim Kakalios is a physicist who explores the hard science

412
00:26:29,340 --> 00:26:32,320
自然科学的物理学家
behind the dreams of science fiction.

413
00:26:32,440 --> 00:26:34,650
他认为他认定了一个
He thinks he has identified

414
00:26:34,760 --> 00:26:38,300
通用的交流媒介
a universal communication medium.

415
00:26:38,410 --> 00:26:40,820
我们都熟悉波
We're all familiar with waves --

416
00:26:40,940 --> 00:26:42,820
声波，光波
sound waves, light waves.

417
00:26:42,940 --> 00:26:46,440
甚至质量也有质量波，或者是水波
Ever matter has waves -- or water waves.

418
00:26:46,550 --> 00:26:48,960
水面的任何扰动
Any disturbance on the surface,

419
00:26:49,080 --> 00:26:51,950
都会引起水面的机械振动
and we get mechanical vibrations on the surface.

420
00:26:52,070 --> 00:26:54,830
分子们连贯地上下起伏
The molecules move up and down but coherently,

421
00:26:54,940 --> 00:26:57,930
形成同心圆，并向外扩张
forming concentric rings that expand outwards.

422
00:26:58,530 --> 00:27:02,710
由于波动遵循可预知的运动规则
Because waves move in predictable and regimented ways,

423
00:27:03,170 --> 00:27:06,660
它可以用来交流数学
they could be used to communicate mathematics,

424
00:27:06,770 --> 00:27:11,500
一种任何文明必然懂得的语言
a language any advanced civilization will surely understand.

425
00:27:12,110 --> 00:27:16,750
但是波并不能像数字一样加减
But waves do not add and subtract like numbers.

426
00:27:18,080 --> 00:27:21,330
我们在数物体的基础上建立了算术
We're used to arithmetic based upon the counting of objects --

427
00:27:21,450 --> 00:27:23,740
一个苹果加上两个苹果，等于三个苹果
one apple and two apples equals three apples.

428
00:27:23,850 --> 00:27:25,840
但是你将基础数学
But if you were to base mathematics

429
00:27:25,950 --> 00:27:27,840
建立在波的相加上
based upon the addition of waves,

430
00:27:27,950 --> 00:27:30,820
比如光波，有时候是正确的
such as light waves, sometimes it works,

431
00:27:30,930 --> 00:27:32,890
有时却会得到混乱的结果
sometimes you get confusing results.

432
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:35,450
现在，我们给数字分配
Here, we've assigned numbers

433
00:27:35,560 --> 00:27:37,300
不同的光的颜色
to the different colors of light.

434
00:27:37,410 --> 00:27:41,290
一是红色，二是橙色，等等
One for red, two for orange, and so on.

435
00:27:41,410 --> 00:27:44,880
如果我把一，红光
If I take a one, red light,

436
00:27:45,390 --> 00:27:48,740
和五，蓝光
and a five, blue light,

437
00:27:48,850 --> 00:27:53,060
相加，我得到了六，紫光
add them together, and I get six, purple light.

438
00:27:53,180 --> 00:27:54,380
这是正确的
That works.

439
00:27:54,630 --> 00:27:55,630
但是这并不总是正确的
But it doesn't always work.

440
00:27:56,490 --> 00:27:58,760
尝试另外一个颜色组合
Let's try another color combination.

441
00:27:58,870 --> 00:28:00,550
一是红色
One is red.

442
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:03,450
三是黄色
Three is yellow.

443
00:28:03,560 --> 00:28:06,350
一加三，应该是四，绿色
One plus three should give us four, green.

444
00:28:06,460 --> 00:28:08,520
但是，我们却得到了橙色，二
But no, we get orange -- two.

445
00:28:08,630 --> 00:28:10,050
一加三等与二？
One plus three equals two?

446
00:28:10,160 --> 00:28:12,130
这就不对了
That doesn't seem right.

447
00:28:12,840 --> 00:28:16,190
尽管波不能像简单的数字一样相加
Even though they do not add up like simple numbers,

448
00:28:16,310 --> 00:28:19,170
它仍然可以通过波
it is still possible to communicate

449
00:28:19,280 --> 00:28:22,570
来交流复杂的数学思想
complex mathematical ideas through waves.

450
00:28:22,680 --> 00:28:26,010
因为无论是水或者光的波动
Because whether they move in water, light,

451
00:28:26,130 --> 00:28:30,750
还是我们周围空气的振动，所有的波都具有频率
or the air around us, all waves have frequencies.

452
00:28:30,870 --> 00:28:33,390
请给我一个C
If you can give me a "C"...

453
00:28:34,640 --> 00:28:35,650
很好
Very good.

454
00:28:35,760 --> 00:28:38,350
一个快活的G#（升G）？
And how about a bouncy "G" sharp?

455
00:28:39,990 --> 00:28:42,990
这两个有区别的频率
Those are two distinct frequencies.

456
00:28:43,710 --> 00:28:46,790
外星人可能描述波的频率时
Aliens may describe the frequency of a wave

457
00:28:46,910 --> 00:28:50,690
使用我们看不懂的数字
with numbers that are indecipherable to us.

458
00:28:51,190 --> 00:28:53,560
但是这两个频率之间的比例
But the ratio between those two frequencies

459
00:28:53,670 --> 00:28:57,130
永远是一致的，无论使用什么样的数字系统
will always be the same, no matter what number system is used.

460
00:28:57,240 --> 00:28:59,370
所以比例可以成为
And the ratios could be the key

461
00:28:59,480 --> 00:29:02,520
两个物种交流的钥匙
to two species expressing their knowledge

462
00:29:02,630 --> 00:29:05,680
交流关于宇宙的基本法则的知识
of the fundamental laws of the universe.

463
00:29:07,200 --> 00:29:10,030
测量一个圆的周长
Measure the circumference of a circle.

464
00:29:10,460 --> 00:29:12,990
然后除以其直径
Now divide by its diameter.

465
00:29:13,550 --> 00:29:16,900
比例是数字π
The ratio is the number pi,

466
00:29:17,020 --> 00:29:20,310
约等于3.14
or about 3.14.

467
00:29:20,760 --> 00:29:24,200
无论你使用什么测量单位
No matter what unit you used to make the measurements --

468
00:29:24,320 --> 00:29:27,490
英寸,或者你的胳膊
inches, your arms...

469
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:28,860
或者其他什么东西
Or anything else --

470
00:29:28,970 --> 00:29:33,160
比例π永远是不变的答案
the ratio pi will always be the answer,

471
00:29:33,870 --> 00:29:40,940
而且π是屈指可数的宇宙基本比例之一
and pi is one of only a handful of fundamental ratios in the universe.

472
00:29:41,050 --> 00:29:43,510
他们一定也会注意到这个
They must certainly have noticed this

473
00:29:43,620 --> 00:29:45,520
因为它是如此共通
because it is so common.

474
00:29:45,630 --> 00:29:50,470
它是我们对于自然认知的基础之一
It is one of the foundations of our understanding of nature.

475
00:29:50,590 --> 00:29:52,640
在第一次接触中
At first contact,

476
00:29:53,020 --> 00:29:56,280
我们可以给外星人发送一对波
we could beam aliens a pair of waves,

477
00:29:56,390 --> 00:30:00,450
它们之间频率的比值等与π
the ratio of their frequencies equal to pi.

478
00:30:00,760 --> 00:30:03,430
我们再发送另一对
We could send another pair

479
00:30:03,540 --> 00:30:08,210
其比率是质子质量
whose ratio is the same as the ratio of the proton mass

480
00:30:08,330 --> 00:30:10,630
和电子质量之比
to the electron mass.

481
00:30:10,900 --> 00:30:15,200
发送这些信息，表明我们懂得
Sending these messages would prove we understand the physics

482
00:30:15,310 --> 00:30:17,540
关于周围宇宙的物理学
of the universe around us.

483
00:30:17,650 --> 00:30:19,660
没有人能确保这会奏效
There's no guarantee it will work,

484
00:30:19,780 --> 00:30:24,570
但是另一种方法，可以寻找一系列变化的频率
but there's another way of looking at a series of changing frequencies.

485
00:30:25,070 --> 00:30:28,080
在地球上，我们称为 音乐
On Earth, we call it music.

486
00:30:33,940 --> 00:30:35,660
如果外星人没有耳朵
If aliens lack ears,

487
00:30:35,780 --> 00:30:38,650
他们也许依然能够欣赏
they might still be able to appreciate the performance

488
00:30:38,760 --> 00:30:41,490
一个高科技摇滚乐队的光电特技
of the high-tech rock band Arcattack,

489
00:30:41,600 --> 00:30:45,960
因为光电特技可以演奏一首歌
because Arcattack can play the same song simultaneously

490
00:30:46,080 --> 00:30:48,620
同时使用声波和光波
in sound waves, light waves,

491
00:30:48,730 --> 00:30:53,050
和嗡嗡放电的一百万伏的特斯拉线圈
and the buzzing discharge of one-million-volt Tesla coils.

492
00:30:53,950 --> 00:30:56,080
特斯拉线圈产生波
A Tesla coil generates waves

493
00:30:56,200 --> 00:30:59,040
产生电磁波信号和电场
both in radio-wave signals and electric fields.

494
00:30:59,150 --> 00:31:02,650
它加热大气产生热
It generates heat by heating up the atmosphere.

495
00:31:02,760 --> 00:31:06,270
产生声和光
It generates sound, and it generates light.

496
00:31:08,470 --> 00:31:10,430
这样的表演可以开启
This performance could initiate

497
00:31:10,540 --> 00:31:15,320
关于我们现存知识的最伟大、和平的交流
the greatest peaceful exchange of knowledge in our existence.

498
00:31:21,260 --> 00:31:24,750
我们可尝试用我们写出的
We could try one of the most beautiful pieces of music

499
00:31:24,860 --> 00:31:26,850
最美丽的音乐片段
that we have developed.

500
00:31:26,960 --> 00:31:29,500
他们也许能够理解
It may just get on their nerves.

501
00:31:30,170 --> 00:31:35,020
但如果外星人不会或不能和我们面对面相见呢？
But what if aliens will not or cannot meet us face-to-face?

502
00:31:35,270 --> 00:31:38,760
还有一种创立接触的机会
There is still a chance to make contact,

503
00:31:38,870 --> 00:31:43,250
外星人可能已经向我们发送了信息，
because aliens could have already sent us a message

504
00:31:43,690 --> 00:31:46,830
信息已经在地球上隐藏了数亿年
hidden on Earth for hundreds of millions of years.

505
00:31:49,310 --> 00:31:51,200
如果把地球的整个历史
If you compress the entire history

506
00:31:51,310 --> 00:31:54,570
压缩进24小时
of planet Earth into 24 hours,

507
00:31:54,760 --> 00:31:58,940
人类只是在最后几秒才出现
human beings would only appear in the final few seconds.

508
00:31:59,150 --> 00:32:01,770
如果外星人早已到过这里
If aliens have already come here,

509
00:32:01,890 --> 00:32:04,610
当时我们可能不在附近，无法迎接他们
we probably were not around to greet them.

510
00:32:04,720 --> 00:32:08,330
但是他们会不会留下造访的记录呢？
But what if they left a record of their visit?

511
00:32:08,800 --> 00:32:11,850
那会在哪里？
Where would it be?

512
00:32:12,440 --> 00:32:15,300
一个科学家觉得，他可以找到它
One scientist thinks he may have found it,

513
00:32:15,740 --> 00:32:17,970
就藏在我们体内
hiding inside us all.

514
00:32:20,240 --> 00:32:22,270
保罗·戴维斯 是一位物理学家、
Paul Davies is a physicist

515
00:32:22,380 --> 00:32:25,850
天体生物学家，他已经花费了数年
and astrobiologist who has spent many years

516
00:32:25,960 --> 00:32:30,460
试图弄清外星人可能如何同我们交流
